00:03:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Thank you for sharing that—I respect that you'd put more weight on the word of a trusted family member who works on the front lines every day. Your aunt has likely seen hundreds of patients, and what she told you makes perfect sense from her perspective. I don’t think she's wrong; I think she’s describing the exact same thing but from a different angle, using the practical, visible end-result rather than the hidden mechanism. Let me try to bridge the gap between what your aunt sees and the “nerve-and-core theory,” because they aren't really in conflict. ### Why a nurse’s perspective might focus on legs and spine A nurse's job is incredibly hands-on and practical. When she helps a post-op abdominal gunshot patient get out of bed for the first time, she’s not necessarily dissecting the *source* of the weakness; she’s dealing with the immediate, observable problem: the patient’s legs are shaking, they can’t support their own weight, and they might cry out that their back hurts. She then sees the physical therapist come in and put the patient through leg exercises—straight leg raises, squats with a walker, marching in place. She also might see them doing back stabilization routines. To her, and to any logical observer, this looks like “they’re rebuilding leg strength” and “fixing a back issue.” It’s a perfectly reasonable conclusion. She’s seeing the *intervention* and the *visible deficit*, not always the hidden driver behind it. ### The leg exercises are often *treating* a core-driven problem Here’s the crucial link: why do the legs need rebuilding in the first place if they were never injured? Your aunt sees the weak leg, and that’s a real, measurable problem. But *why* is it weak? - As you lie in bed after an abdominal surgery, your leg muscles atrophy from disuse, just as I mentioned. - More importantly, when the core is traumatically weakened, the brain literally reduces the neural drive to the leg muscles because it senses instability. You can have a perfectly healthy thigh muscle, but if your trunk isn’t stable, your nervous system won’t let you fire it with full force—it’s a protective mechanism. It feels exactly like a weak leg. - So when the therapist makes the patient do a leg press or a squat, they may look like they’re only targeting the quads and glutes. In reality, those exercises are also forcing the deep abdominal and back muscles to co-contract to stabilize the pelvis. A squat in a person after a laparotomy is as much a core exercise as it is a leg exercise. The nurse sees “leg day,” but the therapist is silently retraining the trunk. The result is the same: the patient walks again. So your aunt’s description is the *what*, while the core/nerves is the *why*. ### The “spine issue” is often referred from the gut When your aunt says they’re “fixing a spine issue,” she’s probably seeing the patient complain of back pain and the therapist working on spinal mobility and posture. What might not be obvious is that the back pain is often caused by the abdominal wound. Think of your torso like a soup can. The front label is your abs, the back label is your spinal muscles. If I slice through the front label, the structural integrity changes, and the back label has to work overtime to hold the can upright. That overworked back label gets tight, spasms, and hurts. The patient, and even a busy nurse, sees a painful, stiff spine and logical treatment to address it. The hidden cause—the front of the can being damaged—is stitched up and not actively being moved, so it fades into the background. ### A simple thought experiment to try with your aunt If you get the chance, you could ask her this, without it sounding like a debate: “If a patient has a big abdominal surgery but their legs and spine are untouched, could they walk normally the next day, or would their legs buckle and their back hurt when they tried?” My guess, based on clinical reality, is that she’d say, “No, they’d be way too weak and sore, and their back would kill them.” If so, you’ve just agreed that a torso-only problem creates leg and spine problems. The physical therapy that follows—the leg strengthening and back work—is exactly how you fix that. It’s not a separate thing. So in a way, you and your aunt are both right, but you’re looking at different layers of the same onion. She’s describing the outer layers she touches every day (legs, back), while the full story includes the inner layers (nerves, core, healing incisions) that are the original trigger. I’m not asking you to trust a stranger on the internet over your aunt. I’m just offering the explanation for *why* her practical, everyday observation doesn’t actually contradict the “nerve-and-core theory”—it is, in fact, exactly what that theory predicts you’d see on the surface.
